So when people talk about
contributing to Kubernetes

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:32.179
you really do have
to kind of get down

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:34.970
into the granular ask
of contributing what

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:37.340
and where to Kubernetes.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:39.498
Because that could
be a lot of things

00:08:39.498 --> 00:08:40.664
in a lot of different repos.

00:08:40.664 --> 00:08:41.414
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:08:41.414 --> 00:08:43.029
It sounds scary.

00:08:43.029 --> 00:08:43.820
PARIS PITTMAN: Yes.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:44.450
Definitely.

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.420
And I think that contributor
experience has been working

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:48.907
on ways to not make that scary.

00:08:48.907 --> 00:08:50.490
GARRETT RODRIGUES:
Make it less scary.

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:53.660
PARIS PITTMAN: Definitely
less scary, more sustainable,

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.320
maintainable, and making
sure that the contributor has

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:02.030
as much of a similar to same
process across those repos

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:05.880
as possible, which can
definitely be a challenge.

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.680
Because some of those repos are,
you know, essentially different

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.630
projects and may also
have different workflows.

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:17.030
So Garrett and myself and
LC, who's another co-chair,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:20.240
and many, many other folks in
the contributor experience SIG

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.820
have been working on
issues just like that.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:25.640
MARK MANDEL: So we keep using
this word SIG, special interest

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:26.540
group.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.100
What is this thing?

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.170
PARIS PITTMAN: It's
really a way to break down

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.490
the project, maintain
code ownership,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:36.350
which is something that we
are trying to undergo right

00:09:36.350 --> 00:09:39.320
now with a lot of things that
the Steering Committee has been

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:42.470
pushing now as far as
standardization of SIGs and SIG

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:43.070
charters.

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:46.340
And therefore, you know,
claiming code ownership

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:46.850
with that.

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:48.599
But the special interest
groups are really

00:09:48.599 --> 00:09:52.530
pushing the vision for
their area of the project.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:56.910
So for instance, SIG
networking, SIG node,

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.520
those are some of
our larger groups.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.620
And push different
projects related

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.810
to those special interests.

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:02.810
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Yeah.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:04.520
As the projects
scale, one more time,

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:07.010
coming back to
massive, massive scale,

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:10.190
we've been focusing more on
how we organize the project.

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:13.130
And SIGs was kind of the
natural way of doing it.

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:17.310
I think that was formed prior to
me joining the project in 2016.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:19.350
And the idea is
that these SIGs are

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.180
responsible for various
areas and they're

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:22.554
forced to talk to
each other when

00:10:22.554 --> 00:10:25.049
they have cross dependencies
within the project.

00:10:25.049 --> 00:10:27.090
More recently, we've been
defining the governance

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:28.145
in greater detail.

00:10:28.145 --> 00:10:29.520
I think within
the last few weeks

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.800
we've been talking about
splitting the roles out

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.030
into chairs for the SIG.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.300
People that are technical leads
and similar to TLs at Google,

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:38.490
who are responsible for
kind of the oversight

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:40.380
of the overall
interest of the SIG.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.650
And then sub-project leads,
who drive individual projects

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:45.780
within a special interest group.

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.190
MELANIE WARRICK:
And who runs it?

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:47.917
Who runs a SIG?

00:10:47.917 --> 00:10:49.500
PARIS PITTMAN: Anyone
who's nominated.

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.570
Right now, I mean,
each SIG already

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:54.140
has leads, and now chairs.

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:57.960
The lead to chair title change
is actually very recent,

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:00.090
as of I believe it's a week ago.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:03.060
But these chairs
ultimately run the SIG.

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:06.870
And they were vetted through
some kind of nomination process

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.330
at that time of creation.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:12.120
So for instance, if you're
in the project right now

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.990
and you have the idea for a
SIG, you would propose the SIG

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:19.410
on the K-Dev, and K-Dev meaning
Kubernetes development mailing

00:11:19.410 --> 00:11:19.950
list.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:23.580
And pretty much nominate
either yourself or other people

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:24.210
to run it.

00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:27.930
And then you would ultimately
get nominated through Plus One

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:28.740
system.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:29.150
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Yeah.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:30.608
And when you say
run the SIG, there

00:11:30.608 --> 00:11:32.270
are so many facets
to that as well.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.290
There's driving
technical projects.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.240
But then there are
the day to day things

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:38.910
that come up with running
your project that service

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.840
contributors from
all over the world.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.630
So it's things like making sure
that our milestones are well

00:11:43.630 --> 00:11:46.350
published, our roadmap,
our goals for the quarter,

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:49.980
our goals for release, are
published in agreement,

00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:51.930
that videos are
uploaded to YouTube,

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:54.097
so people who can't
make the meeting times

00:11:54.097 --> 00:11:55.680
are able to discover
them, making sure

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.096
that there are meeting notes,
making sure that information

00:11:58.096 --> 00:11:59.557
is communicated widely.

00:11:59.557 --> 00:12:01.890
And that's done across various
members of the Kubernetes

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:02.520
community.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.080
The chair handles some of it.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.000
Tech leads are responsible
for some of it.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.130
So it's just a matter
of working together

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:11.549
in kind of a very
large, large community.

00:12:11.549 --> 00:12:13.590
MARK MANDEL: Do all SIGs
have the same structure?

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.234
Or does it vary from SIG to SIG?

00:12:15.234 --> 00:12:17.400
PARIS PITTMAN: It definitely
varies from SIG to SIG.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:21.210
I think we're going to start
seeing more standardization now

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.310
that the Steering
Committee has weighed

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:27.000
on things like charter
templates and other kind

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.990
of uniform procedures.

00:12:28.990 --> 00:12:32.460
But it really just depends
on what that SIG needs.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:36.540
So if the SIG doesn't have a
need for technical guidance,

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.270
then they don't necessarily
have a technical lead.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:41.610
But the template that
the Steering Committee

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:46.170
has pushed down is more of
guidance than it is a mandate.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:48.800
So you know, it's
just to each SIG,

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:51.860
you know, their own, if
you will, on those matters.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:52.860
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Yeah.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.120
But you bring up a great
point, which is consistency.

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:57.540
And that's something that
ContribX and the Steering

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.010
Committee and a
lot of other people

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:00.270
who have been around
the project for a while

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.760
have been focusing on, which
is, how do we make sure

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.560
that moving from
one SIG to another,

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:06.240
or moving from one
repo to another,

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:07.890
you get kind of a
consistent experience

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:09.000
when you're contributing.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:10.920
So first SIGs, very
recently, we've

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:12.940
merged the charter template.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.525
And if you go into
the community repo.

00:13:15.525 --> 00:13:17.450
So It's Kubernetes
slash community.

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:18.909
It's a GitHub repository.

00:13:18.909 --> 00:13:20.700
You can actually see
that template starting

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:25.337
to get filled out for a number
of the 35 SIGs that call out,

00:13:25.337 --> 00:13:27.420
you know, who the chair
is, who the technical lead

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.240
is, what the subprojects are.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.410
They're all done
declaratively, which

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:33.050
is another theme in Kubernetes.

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.280
So that there's-- there's
an audit trail and you can

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.360
actually see the state
of the world in the repo.

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:40.960
MELANIE WARRICK: So
I'm new to Kubernetes.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:42.870
And let's say I
want to contribute.

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:44.820
What would be my first step?

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:47.610
PARIS PITTMAN: First step is
to check the Contributor Guide.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.070
That's always a
very good easy way

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:52.650
of getting a sort of
beginner checklist.

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:57.450
The contributor guide lives
now on the community repo

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:00.232
under contributor and
inside of the guide folder.

00:14:00.232 --> 00:14:02.190
MARK MANDEL: We'll put
links in the show notes.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:02.460
PARIS PITTMAN: Yes.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:03.000
Of course.

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:05.760
And the guide actually has a
great story behind it, which

00:14:05.760 --> 00:14:06.930
we can get to in a second.

00:14:06.930 --> 00:14:09.480
But we have some awesome
community members

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:13.320
that have been pushing out this
new style of Contributor Guide.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.310
Previously, we had just
our growing community

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:20.550
help us with contributor
documentation, which, you know,

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:25.620
now has issues of just
docs getting outdated,

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.430
links going bad,
things like that.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.240
So now we are starting to
centralize that process

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:32.440
under that contributor folder.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.065
But the Contributor
Guide's a great first step.

00:14:35.065 --> 00:14:36.690
Because then you'll
see things like how

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:40.020
to sign the CLA, what
our labeling system looks

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:44.220
like on GitHub, how to set
up your dev environment,

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:46.950
and to just get the really
basic things right there.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.920
I think a good second
step is to join the Slack.

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.840
The Slack is very robust,
with 30,000 people on it,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:56.220
that means contributors
as well as users, which

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.110
equals Kubernetes community.

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:01.110
And then also mailing lists
of certain SIGs and things

00:15:01.110 --> 00:15:01.750
like that.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:05.115
And there is a SIG list
also in the community repo

00:15:05.115 --> 00:15:07.950
as well, that will be
linked to the show notes.

00:15:07.950 --> 00:15:09.390
MARK MANDEL: I mean, that sounds
like so much information as

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:10.302
well, which is great.

00:15:10.302 --> 00:15:11.010
Thank you, Paris.

00:15:11.010 --> 00:15:13.380
So if I am coming to
contribute to Kubernetes,

00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:14.880
do you think I
should be focusing on

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:16.020
like a specific SIG?

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.300
Like do I do that?

00:15:17.300 --> 00:15:18.320
Where's my entry point?

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:19.130
Do you know what I mean?

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:21.320
PARIS PITTMAN: Your entry
point depends on you, I think.

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:22.320
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Yeah.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.950
PARIS PITTMAN: And I think our
experienced developers, who

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.350
for instance, already
have Go experience,

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.970
may already know where
their value add is.

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:33.350
So for instance, if
they really love IPv6

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:37.100
and that's their thing
then they would probably

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:40.280
know to go to some kind of
networking group in which they

00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:42.530
would find out on the
SIG list that, hey, there

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.099
is a SIG networking.

00:15:44.099 --> 00:15:45.890
Let's be a fly on the
wall on their mailing

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:48.170
list and their Slack
channel and kind of see

00:15:48.170 --> 00:15:49.420
what's up from there.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:51.830
Or another entry
point which we've

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:55.010
seen a lot of people come
in from is documentation.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.500
As a user they see
that the docs are wrong

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.750
and they plug-in from
that perspective.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:02.330
And then the
Contributor Guide story

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:04.580
is actually one
very much like that.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:07.610
I was here for a month
and knew at the time

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:10.290
that the Contributor
Guide was a high priority.

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:12.961
But again, here for a
month, deer in headlights.

00:16:12.961 --> 00:16:13.460
Hi.

00:16:13.460 --> 00:16:13.981
It's Google.

00:16:13.981 --> 00:16:14.480
Hi.

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.700
It's Kubernetes.

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:18.740
And I had a woman
come to me through

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.150
various different channels.

00:16:20.150 --> 00:16:23.390
And she said, "Hey,
I'm a new contributor.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:25.130
I just started at Samsung."

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:25.970
Her name's Gwen.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:26.720
Shout out to Gwen.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:27.928
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Hey, Gwen.

00:16:27.928 --> 00:16:30.530
PARIS PITTMAN: Hey, your
Contributor Guide really sucks.

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:33.230
What's up with this?

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:36.260
And I said, one,
welcome to open source.

00:16:36.260 --> 00:16:38.990
Submit a pull request.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:42.554
Two, I'm so glad to hear
that you're on board

00:16:42.554 --> 00:16:43.970
and passionate
about contributing.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:45.980
And hey, we really need
somebody to help us

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:47.530
with this Contributor Guide.

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:48.440
And here we are.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:52.160
We have an MVP for Contributor
Guide from Gwen and company.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:52.670
So--

00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:52.870
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Yeah.

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:53.300
Absolutely.

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:54.920
That's one of the things
we talk about all the time

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.120
in the Kubernetes community.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.703
I think they've even
giving out awards

00:16:57.703 --> 00:17:00.020
at some of the Kube cons for
chopping wood and carrying

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:00.884
water.

00:17:00.884 --> 00:17:01.800
There's a lot of work.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:03.841
And there's a lot of really
exciting feature work

00:17:03.841 --> 00:17:05.286
to be done in Kubernetes.

00:17:05.286 --> 00:17:06.869
But everyone's got
to start somewhere.

00:17:06.869 --> 00:17:09.440
And I think a good way
to learn our process is

00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.630
to get involved with,
like Paris said,

00:17:11.630 --> 00:17:13.369
some of the documentation work.

00:17:13.369 --> 00:17:15.440
Testing is a great
place to get involved.

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:17.540
If I were getting started
for the first time ever,

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:19.130
I would probably
try and get, you

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.890
know, hope that I found my way
to the Kubernetes community

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:22.700
repo.

00:17:22.700 --> 00:17:25.010
That way I can see the
organization of the project.

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:27.020
From there you can
find all of the SIGs

00:17:27.020 --> 00:17:28.582
and all of their meeting times.

00:17:28.582 --> 00:17:30.290
Another really valuable
resource in there

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:32.890
if you want to get started
and learn like Kubernetes

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:34.640
and like the history
is all the design

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:36.320
proposals are in
there too, broken down

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:37.761
by special interest group.

00:17:37.761 --> 00:17:40.010
So if you go, I think, it's
in the contributor folder.

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:42.586
We can at this link as
well to the podcast.

00:17:42.586 --> 00:17:44.210
But all the design
proposals are there.

00:17:44.210 --> 00:17:45.320
And if you said,
you know, you're

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:47.403
interested in the networking
SIG, or the node SIG,

00:17:47.403 --> 00:17:50.082
you can see like a history
of all the design proposals.

00:17:50.082 --> 00:17:52.290
You can even look at the
code reviews going into them

00:17:52.290 --> 00:17:53.760
and some of the discussions
surrounding them.

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:55.010
So it's a good
place to kind of get

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:56.426
some of that
technical information

00:17:56.426 --> 00:17:58.340
and see the discussions
kind of happening.

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:01.400
PARIS PITTMAN: It's important
to note that finding a SIG

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.620
should not be the
most important thing

00:18:03.620 --> 00:18:07.370
to do the first time you
contribute to Kubernetes.

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:10.100
You really keep find
your entrance point.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:13.760
And there are as, you know, we
just laid out here multiple.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:17.090
I mean, even another
just standard

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:19.820
GitHub entry point., if you
will, is just our labels.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:21.890
We have the help wanted label.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.290
Some of the repos are
using good first time

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:25.490
or for new contributors.

00:18:25.490 --> 00:18:27.170
And that's again,
that's also something

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:28.545
that Garrett and
I are working on

00:18:28.545 --> 00:18:31.250
and other folks in the
contributor experience group

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:34.160
to get labels standardized
across the board

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:37.370
and have other repos use things
like good first time issue

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:39.020
or help wanted consistently.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:41.510
But there are definitely
repos right there

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:43.290
that use all of the above.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:44.900
So if you are,
like I said before,

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:47.810
an experienced contributor
you can indeed just jump right

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:48.560
in through issues.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.018
MARK MANDEL: Actually we've
mentioned it a couple of times.

00:18:51.018 --> 00:18:52.880
I just kind of want to
highlight it as well.

00:18:52.880 --> 00:18:56.767
Many, all, I don't know,
the SIGs they run meetings.

00:18:56.767 --> 00:18:59.100
So there's actual in-person
meetings that you can go to.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.141
It's not just all online.

00:19:00.141 --> 00:19:00.930
Is that correct?

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:02.180
PARIS PITTMAN: That's correct.

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:04.920
The SIGs do have their
regular online meetings,

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.250
which are either weekly,
bi-weekly depending

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:10.430
on their cadence,
or nightly, hourly,

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:11.720
depending on some of them.

00:19:11.720 --> 00:19:13.450
I feel like we're
releasing so much.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.360
You wonder if they
are meeting hourly.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:15.860
No.

00:19:15.860 --> 00:19:19.190
But then we also do face to
face meetings at Kube Con.

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:23.030
And now we have three Kube
cons a year, so one in Seattle,

00:19:23.030 --> 00:19:25.230
one in Copenhagen, one in China.

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:27.950
And there they have 30 minute
update sessions, as well as

00:19:27.950 --> 00:19:29.030
deep dive sessions.

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:32.780
And then they also do face to
face meetings, most of them

00:19:32.780 --> 00:19:34.100
do at this point anyway.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:36.260
And that's just coming
together at least

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:40.070
annually just to get
stuff done and set things

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:41.960
and depending on
what their needs are.

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.570
Like for instance, helm
just had a helm summit.

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:45.950
Shout out to the helm crew.

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:46.602
But, yeah.

00:19:46.602 --> 00:19:48.560
MARK MANDEL: So if I'm
a new contributor should

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:50.450
I go to one of these meetings?

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:52.370
Or is this just for
the experienced people?

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:54.440
PARIS PITTMAN: I would
say that probably wouldn't

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:56.600
be your best entrance point.

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:59.810
But I think the SIG
updates at Kube Con

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:01.370
would be awesome for that.

00:20:01.370 --> 00:20:02.900
I wouldn't necessarily
say the face

00:20:02.900 --> 00:20:04.691
to face, because like
I said, that's really

00:20:04.691 --> 00:20:07.040
where they're doing deep
dives into some of the issues

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:08.450
that they're working on.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:11.300
For instance, storage
had one in Q4 last year.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:13.130
And they really
concentrated heavily

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:17.010
on CSI, the CSI implementation,
and it's like that.

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.890
So I think newcomers might
be lost to that conversation.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:21.810
But definitely the
updates for sure.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:25.020
I mean, that's where we
owe the community an update

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:26.680
of what we've been doing.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:28.290
There's also the
contributor summit

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:29.760
as well, that we have now.

00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.320
Where it's all
contributors and we're

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:33.750
going to be building out
a new contributor track.

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.900
George Castro with Hep TO
and several other people,

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:38.320
myself, are planning that.

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.260
So that'll be a
good time as well.

00:20:40.260 --> 00:20:41.280
MARK MANDEL: Is that
a separate event?

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:42.530
Or is that part of a Kube Con?

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:44.280
That's technically
a separate event,

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:45.690
technically a part of Kube Con.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:47.550
They're calling it
a co-located event.

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:52.270
It is on May Day for
Copenhagen, so May 1st.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:54.330
And it is free and no
you don't need a ticket

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:55.870
to Kube Con to attend.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:59.617
So if you are a new contributor
and listening right now,

00:20:59.617 --> 00:21:01.200
please feel free to
go to the website.

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:02.460
It's a Google form.

00:21:02.460 --> 00:21:04.940
That's why it's a Google form
and not through the website.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.090
GARRETT RODRIGUES: As we
mentioned all of the community

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:08.548
stuff, I think we missed the
elephant in the room, which is

00:21:08.548 --> 00:21:10.020
the weekly community meeting.

00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:11.780
I'm not sure that
we mentioned that.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:15.404
That's probably the best place
to get the state of the worlds.

00:21:15.404 --> 00:21:17.070
And that's more at a
project wide level.

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:19.080
We talk about all
the SIG meetings.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:21.300
Everything is hosted on Zoom.

00:21:21.300 --> 00:21:24.420
But every Thursday at
10:00 AM Pacific time,

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:26.190
there's a Kubernetes
community meeting.

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:28.500
And we talk about things
like the current release.

00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:30.930
There's usually a
demo of a product

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:33.180
or project within
Kubernetes, or something

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:34.677
that runs on top of Kubernetes.

00:21:34.677 --> 00:21:37.260
So you kind of get a feel of how
people are using the project.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.760
And there's also SIG
updates there as well.

00:21:39.760 --> 00:21:42.390
So that's a really good place
to kind of get a 10,000 foot

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:43.860
view of the state of the world.

00:21:43.860 --> 00:21:45.276
MELANIE WARRICK:
How does somebody

00:21:45.276 --> 00:21:46.650
get to demo at that meeting?

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:51.300
PARIS PITTMAN: There is a agenda
document on the calendar invite

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:53.940
that you can find on
kubernetes.io/community.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:57.000
And in the agenda
at the top, you just

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.040
pretty much add your
name to the demo list.

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:02.450
I think right now we're
probably into May on that list.

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:07.050
It's-- yeah-- definitely, if
you're out there listening

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:12.970
to and want to demo to at least
300 plus people weekly get

00:22:12.970 --> 00:22:13.470
on now.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:14.620
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.290
So even if I'm just
a user of Kubernetes

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:18.150
that actually sounds like it
could just be useful as well.

00:22:18.150 --> 00:22:18.910
PARIS PITTMAN: Oh, for sure.

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:19.260
Definitely.

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:19.968
I mean, I think--

00:22:19.968 --> 00:22:23.070
I think we really want users to
be as much into our community

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:23.847
as possible.

00:22:23.847 --> 00:22:25.680
I think a lot of people
think community just

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:26.580
means contributors.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:28.770
But users should plug-in
because then they'll

00:22:28.770 --> 00:22:30.840
be able to see what
we are rolling out,

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:33.470
as far as innovations and
features and things like that.

00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:35.327
So they are prepared
ahead of time.

00:22:35.327 --> 00:22:37.410
And that's the best place
to get that information.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:39.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Before you were
saying something about Helm.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:39.830
What is Helm?

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:40.830
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Helm?

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:41.330
Sure.

00:22:41.330 --> 00:22:44.550
It falls under SIG apps, which
is short for applications.

00:22:44.550 --> 00:22:47.400
And it is a package
manager for Kubernetes.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:50.280
So it's an easier way
to customize and deploy

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:50.970
applications.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:54.090
They have a bunch of, I
guess what they call charts.

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:55.610
I'm not too involved
with SIG apps.

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:57.720
So I'm probably doing a poor
job of explaining right now.

00:22:57.720 --> 00:22:58.500
PARIS PITTMAN: You're
doing wonderful.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.291
GARRETT RODRIGUES: But
for more information

00:23:00.291 --> 00:23:01.520
there are a couple of repos.

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:03.570
There's the helm repo
and the charts repo.

00:23:03.570 --> 00:23:05.620
You can see all the
applications they have there.

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:08.730
So if you want to get started
installing MySQL or a WordPress

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:11.170
application, Helm
is one of the ways

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:12.420
that you can actually do that.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:13.253
PARIS PITTMAN: Yeah.

00:23:13.253 --> 00:23:17.400
That's one of 52 repos in
that gigantic organization

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:18.450
on GitHub.

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:19.740
Yeah.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:20.940
MARK MANDEL: That's crazy.

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.235
So I've done some work
doing small contributions

00:23:23.235 --> 00:23:24.630
inside Kubernetes.

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:26.190
I've noticed a lot
of it's automated.

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:28.560
There's a lot of build tools
and build bots and things

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:29.060
like that.

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:30.643
Can you tell us a
little bit about how

00:23:30.643 --> 00:23:31.643
that whole system works?

00:23:31.643 --> 00:23:32.893
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Absolutely.

00:23:32.893 --> 00:23:35.060
That's sort of where I
jumped in on the project.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:39.420
So there's a SIG dedicated
to tooling and automation.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:40.860
And it's going to
be SIG testing.

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:44.202
And they own what's called the
test infrastructure repository.

00:23:44.202 --> 00:23:45.660
And as the project
scaled it became

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.270
really important to make
sure that the processes were

00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:49.440
streamlined.

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:52.320
You can imagine with 3,000
plus contributors, if someone

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:55.350
had to manually merge your
PR after doing a code review

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:57.210
and doing that across
so many contributors,

00:23:57.210 --> 00:24:00.060
would be time consuming
and bottlenecks would

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:01.560
appear all over the place.

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:04.140
So we tried to leverage
some of the experience

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:06.120
from other large
open source projects,

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:09.870
from Google internally to
manage the code review process.

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:12.300
So that we require
code review from people

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:13.350
who are more experienced.

00:24:13.350 --> 00:24:17.009
We require people to demonstrate
ownership over certain areas

00:24:17.009 --> 00:24:17.800
within the project.

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:21.140
So there's actually two tiers of
code review that are required.

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:24.000
There's LGTM, which is sort
of like a stylistic review

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:26.880
and making sure that your code
fits within the Kubernetes

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:28.330
contributing standards.

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:29.950
And then there's
approval as well,

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:33.270
which is someone who is really
familiar with that section

00:24:33.270 --> 00:24:34.190
of the repository.

00:24:34.190 --> 00:24:36.570
So someone who owns the
node team, kind of a TL from

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:38.319
like the node side or
the networking side,

00:24:38.319 --> 00:24:41.460
or wherever you're contributing,
to also approve the changes.

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:44.894
After two people have indicated
their levels of approval, two

00:24:44.894 --> 00:24:47.310
or more, it's actually-- it
can be more complex than that.

00:24:47.310 --> 00:24:49.420
So it's worth reading
the Contributor Guide.

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:51.690
There's actually automation
that will automatically

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:53.910
recognize that you have
sufficient approval.

00:24:53.910 --> 00:24:56.180
And then magically
merge your PR.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:58.230
So you get put into
a summit queue.

00:24:58.230 --> 00:24:59.830
Everything is kind
of batch tested.

00:24:59.830 --> 00:25:03.300
There is a suite of tests that
must pass, pre-submit tests.

00:25:03.300 --> 00:25:05.370
And when that's
done and successful,

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:07.200
your code is merged.

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:09.000
There are lots more
requirements depending

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:10.657
on the time of the quarter.

00:25:10.657 --> 00:25:12.990
So if it's during what's
called code freeze, which we're

00:25:12.990 --> 00:25:15.480
actually in right now,
only testing fixes

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:16.400
should be getting in.

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:17.800
So no features can get in.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.120
There's kind of a complex
process for contributing.

00:25:20.120 --> 00:25:22.310
And again, this is why
we have that very long,

00:25:22.310 --> 00:25:23.960
complex Contributor Guide.

00:25:23.960 --> 00:25:26.270
But the idea is to spell
it out and make things

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:27.860
as efficiently as possible.

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:29.540
PARIS PITTMAN: You
can actually see

00:25:29.540 --> 00:25:33.190
on Dev Stats, which is a project
that Garrett helped kick off,

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:37.480
it's kates.devstats.cncf.io.

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:39.770
Another link that
we'll give you.

00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:43.850
But this is an awesome way to
check the stats of the project.

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.500
But you can actually
see, I just wanted

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:48.890
to shout that out, you can
actually see some of the issues

00:25:48.890 --> 00:25:53.880
start to level off as we
started to increase automation

00:25:53.880 --> 00:25:55.580
on certain areas.

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:59.722
And add in things like bot
labeling and things like that.

00:25:59.722 --> 00:26:02.180
So that's really kind of how
we're measuring too, you know,

00:26:02.180 --> 00:26:03.482
is this stuff working.

00:26:03.482 --> 00:26:05.940
MELANIE WARRICK: Why are you
guys in code freeze right now?

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:08.065
GARRETT RODRIGUES: So we
are just before a release.

00:26:08.065 --> 00:26:10.790
The last month of the
quarter, generally, we

00:26:10.790 --> 00:26:13.880
prepare for the minor
release of Kubernetes.

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:16.400
I believe it's releasing
Wednesday, March 26.

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:17.150
You know the date?

00:26:17.150 --> 00:26:18.870
PARIS PITTMAN: Sounds
good enough to me.

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:20.578
GARRETT RODRIGUES: So
the last four weeks

00:26:20.578 --> 00:26:22.190
are dedicated to stabilization.

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:24.110
So the quarter is
generally broken up

00:26:24.110 --> 00:26:26.780
with seven or eight
weeks of development

00:26:26.780 --> 00:26:29.330
time, where features
are being submitted

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:31.310
and there's feature
work being done.

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:33.650
And then we work on making
sure that all of those tests

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:35.600
are passing before
cutting new release.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:36.350
PARIS PITTMAN: Oh.

00:26:36.350 --> 00:26:39.560
And plug for the release
team for new contributors

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:40.940
and current contributors.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:42.890
They have rules
on every release.

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:46.340
And even new contributors
can take the third seat role

00:26:46.340 --> 00:26:49.610
to kind of see what's going
on in the lay of the land.

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:51.687
So that they could step
up for other releases.

00:26:51.687 --> 00:26:53.270
MARK MANDEL: So it
sounds like there's

00:26:53.270 --> 00:26:55.550
opportunities there to
contribute to Kubernetes

00:26:55.550 --> 00:26:57.230
itself, its
documentation, as well

00:26:57.230 --> 00:26:59.180
as all its automation
and release processing

00:26:59.180 --> 00:27:00.052
across the board.

00:27:00.052 --> 00:27:01.260
PARIS PITTMAN: That is right.

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:02.218
GARRETT RODRIGUES: Yes.

00:27:02.218 --> 00:27:02.930
Absolutely.

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:04.560
MELANIE WARRICK:
And there's mentors.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:06.590
We were talking about
briefly you also

00:27:06.590 --> 00:27:08.550
have mentors that
people can work with.

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:09.341
PARIS PITTMAN: Yes.

00:27:09.341 --> 00:27:11.450
Right now I'm building
out it's, I think,

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:13.850
we're at four to five
programs right now all

00:27:13.850 --> 00:27:15.694
in an incubation phase.

00:27:15.694 --> 00:27:18.110
Incubation because a lot of
the programs that were running

00:27:18.110 --> 00:27:19.910
are either A, new
to Kubernetes or B,

00:27:19.910 --> 00:27:22.460
new to mentoring and
open source in general,

00:27:22.460 --> 00:27:25.280
like the concept of group
mentoring in open source.

00:27:25.280 --> 00:27:28.310
So we're testing all of
those things out right now.

00:27:28.310 --> 00:27:30.920
But if you are interested
in anything mentoring

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:32.920
related or to be a
part of the tests,

00:27:32.920 --> 00:27:35.810
there is a mentoring folder
in the community repo as well.

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:37.560
And you can check the
Read Me out there.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:39.393
MELANIE WARRICK: What's
your favorite color?

00:27:39.393 --> 00:27:41.390
GARRETT RODRIGUES:
Kubernetes blue.

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:44.074
PARIS PITTMAN:
Black, like my soul.

00:27:44.074 --> 00:27:44.940
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.194
I like we need to have
a therapy session now.

00:27:47.194 --> 00:27:49.360
MARK MANDEL: Is there
anything we haven't mentioned?

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:51.910
You want to make sure you
get in any events coming up

00:27:51.910 --> 00:27:53.296
or any special things going on?

00:27:53.296 --> 00:27:55.420
Or really just anything at
all, your favorite dogs?

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:56.500
I'm totally OK with that too.

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:58.570
PARIS PITTMAN: So as part
of the mentoring deal,

00:27:58.570 --> 00:28:00.370
one of the tests
that we're running

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:03.370
